From 4adee5ed1009a0d1be19a42b891315b1a186d8e9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fredrik Hult <fredrik.hult@outlook.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] Kitty replaces what is sent again, deletes frames, and keeps its byte count true

Four places where Kitty's graphics did not do what the specification (or, where it is silent,
kitty) does.

A placement sent again under the same image and placement ids replaces the first ("the second one
will replace the first"); it was added beside it, so a client moving or resizing a picture left
the old one behind. Image data transmitted again under an id takes the old image's placements off
the screen, as the specification asks; they stayed. An image number still makes a new image and
leaves the older ones shown.

d=f and d=F deleted nothing, from when no frames were stored. They now delete one frame as kitty
does: r, the first by default, the last at most. The frames after it move down a number, the
current one with them; with one left the animation stops, and that last frame is the picture, which
d=f keeps and d=F deletes with the image.

The registry gave back an image's size at the time it was removed, not what it was charged. An
animated image counted its root twice (the root frame shares the image's array) and an edited root
was a copy never charged, so each one removed left the total lower -- below zero in the end, where
the budget no longer evicts anything. The registry now records each image's charge, a deleted
frame's bytes are refunded, and an animation no longer counts a shared root.
